\par\par \b \uc2  HK$ Bonds Mixed On HK Dollar Weakness, U.S. Treasury Rally \b0\par\par\uc2 221 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Juli 1997\par 12:01\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1997, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  HONG KONG (AP-Dow Jones)--Hong Kong dollar bond prices were mixed in late trading Wednesday, weighed down on one end by more fears of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and buoyed on the other by an overnight rally in U.S. Treasurys, traders said. \par \par \uc2  Short-term bills endured another session of selling, especially in the afternoon, as fears intensifed that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  'People are just worried about the Hong Kong dollar being challenged by American funds. That's why bill (prices) went down 40 to 50 ticks,' a trader at a U.S. brokerage house said. 'But the longer-period bonds remain unchanged.' \par \par \uc2  Late Wednesday, the one-week Exchange Fund bill yield rose to 6.48% from 6.34% Tuesday. The 10-year Exchange Fund bond yield fell to 6.78%, compared with 6.99% Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar is quoted at HK$7.7475, up from HK$7.7458 Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  A depreciating Hong Kong dollar drives investors out of the bond markets on fears the value of their assets will be significantly eroded. However, the activity usually centers around bills, which are much more sensitive to short-term volatility. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 23-07-97 \par \par \uc2  1001GMT \par \par \uc2  4178 5096 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011006dt7n05h5c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
