\par\par \b \uc2  HK Dollar Stronger;U.S. Dlr Sold On  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Fears \b0\par\par\uc2 316 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Januar 1997\par 04:37\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1997, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 HONG KONG\b0 \highlight\uc2  (AP-Dow Jones)--The \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar has moved to stronger levels Friday after the U.S. dollar met with stiff resistance in the previous session. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar has traded between $7.7374 (Hong Kong) and HK$7.7376 so far Friday. It is quoted at HK$7.7374 at 0325 GMT, down from HK$7.7391 around the same time Thursday. \par \par \uc2  A \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer at a North American bank said a number of banks may have been a bit nervous Thursday as the U.S. dollar appeared ready to break through the HK$7.7400 level. \par \par \uc2  Most banks - domestic and overseas - had been seen buying back short U.S. dollar positions in the first full trading week of the new year, the dealer said. \par \par \uc2  The banks, including his own, preferred holding long U.S. dollar positions to benefit from an interest rate differential that favors the U.S. dollar. Their activity took the U.S. dollar to a high of HK$7.7397 Thursday from HK$7.7335 to open 1997. \par \par \uc2  But as the U.S. dollar approached HK$7.7400, overseas investors, particularly those in Singapore, seemed keen to square their long positions, perhaps remembering that the Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  began 'checking prices' with banks the last time the U.S. dollar marched up to HK$7.7400. \par \par \uc2  There was no sign of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Thursday, but the specter of queries on the reasons for price quotes was enough to spark U.S. dollar selling. \par \par \uc2  After the selling moved the U.S. dollar down to HK$7.7380, stop-loss orders kicked in, and the U.S. dollar slipped all the way down to HK$7.7365 Thursday, before recovering to above HK$7.7370 early Friday. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 10-01-97 \par \par \uc2  0337GMT \par \par \uc2  1053 4178 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011006dt1a005ic\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
