\par\par \b \uc2  Japan/Bilateral Repo Accords -3: HK Welcomes Accord \b0\par\par\uc2 211 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 April 1996\par 07:12\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1996, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Hong Kong set up repo agreements with Australia, China, Indonesia, Malaysia, the Philippines and Thailand. \par \par \uc2  In a statement after the agreement Thursday with Japan, Joseph Yam, chief executive of the Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said, the latest accord 'would enhance cooperation between Asian central banks in their efforts to maintain \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  stability. This would also improve liquidity of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves of central banks.' \par \par \uc2  The Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  said that entering in the secured agreements with central banks whose credit standings are generally higher than those of the private sector banks would improve liquidity with minimal risk. \par \par \uc2  The Asian monetary authorities have been in close contact since the Mexican peso crisis a year ago prompted them to begin discussing means of providing each other liquidity when necessary to ensure \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market stability. \par \par \uc2  Japan had expressed interest in joining the arrangements but hadn't until now formalized any agreement. In an effort to promote \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market stability, however, it announced an agreement in February with monetary authorities of \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Singapore under which \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Singapore could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in dollar-yen trading in their markets on behalf of the Bank of Japan. \par \par \uc2  (END) AP-DOW JONES 