\par\par \b \uc2  HK dollar vulnerable to speculation -- Schroders. \b0\par\par\uc2 502 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 August 1995\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1995 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  HONG KONG, Aug 23 (Reuter) - The possibility of a run on the Hong Kong dollar by speculators is higher than the government thinks, Schroder Securities economist Dong Tao said in a recent report. \par \par \uc2  "We believe there are many factors which place Hong Kong under speculators' scrutiny, even if it is not the weakest economy in the world," he said. \par \par \uc2  Some of these include a weakening economy in which the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is fixed to a bigger and rebounding economy and the 1997 handover to China with its uncertainties. Since 1983, the Hong Kong dollar has been pegged at 7.8 to the U.S. dollar. \par \par \uc2  Regarding Hong Kong's fundamentals, Tao said the gross domestic product growth gap between the colony and the U.S. is shrinking while the inflation gap remains wide. \par \par \uc2  "More importantly, Hong Kong's balance of goods and services is forecast to turn to a deficit in 1996 for the first time since the introduction of the peg system," he said in a telephone interview. \par \par \uc2  He said the Hong Kong government argues that because Hong Kong does not have the weakest economy in the world, its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not a likely target for speculators. \par \par \uc2  "We feel the possibility of some speculation against the Hong Kong dollar is a lot bigger than the government claims," Tao said. \par \par \uc2  He said reasons why Hong Kong might be a target include the fact that Hong Kong residents can easily switch between local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  accounts, with very low transaction costs. \par \par \uc2  Also, while the Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  has an estimated US$53 billion in foreign reserves with which to defend the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the colony's defacto \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has limited connections with other major central banks. \par \par \uc2  The HKMA's limited contacts with other central banks would make any effort at joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem a run on the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar difficult and slow "if in fact any support would arrive at all," he said. \par \par \uc2  In addition, Tao said a remote but real threat exists of externally generated "panic" selling of the Hong Kong dollar, for example as the result of unexpected political upheaval in China. \par \par \uc2  Despite these issues, he said the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely to remain stable, particularly given the HKMA's large foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  cushion. \par \par \uc2  In his report, Tao said if a run on the Hong Kong dollar were to occur, there is a 75 percent chance the HKMA would successfully defend the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  by using its reserves. \par \par \uc2  The other possibility is that the speculation would trigger capital outflows by local and international fund managers, forcing the HKMA to raise short-term interest rates. \par \par \uc2  If higher rates did not stem the run, the HKMA would either re-peg the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to the U.S. dollar, to a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , to the yuan or let it float freely. \par \par \uc2  Tao said a re-peg to the U.S. dollar is the most likely outcome.--Hong Kong newsroom, (852) 2843-6441 \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1995 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011103dr8n04s7m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\pic