\par\par \b \uc2  HK Dollar Sliding/China's Deng -3:  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  Not Seen \b0\par\par\uc2 143 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 April 1995\par 09:09\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1995, Dow Jones & Co., Inc.) \par \par \uc2  A senior manager with an Asian bank says the U.S. dollar was driven up to levels around HK$7.7395 as speculators 'bought up the dollar' on the rumors about Deng Xiaoping's health. \par \par \uc2  'Basically, this rumor is used all the time,' the manager says. \par \par \uc2  He says the dollar's move back to levels around HK$7.7350 came on profit-taking and that he doesn't see any \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the territory's de facto \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  'I haven't seen any special action by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,' he says. \par \par \uc2  Despite the spurt of rumor-related activity in the usually quiet local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, the manager says 'we can say trading is still within normal parameters.' \par \par \uc2  (END) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 25-04-95 \par \par \uc2  0709GMT \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011025dr4p02y8p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
