\par\par \b \uc2  HK Dollar Slips On Arbitrage-Driven U.S. Dollar Buying \b0\par\par\uc2 326 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 1995\par 05:15\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1995, Dow Jones & Co., Inc.) \par \par \uc2  HONG KONG (AP-DJ)--Arbitrage-driven U.S. dollar buying is squeezing the Hong Kong dollar to slightly weaker levels on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  So far Thursday, the dollar, also called the 'TT,' has traded between $7.7317 (Hong Kong) and HK$7.7325. \par \par \uc2  At 0354 GMT, the dollar is quoted at HK$7.7324, up from HK$7.7322 around the same time Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The treasurer at a U.K.-based bank says dollar buying has picked up on Thursday because shorter-term Hong Kong dollar rates remain well below comparable U.S. dollar rates. \par \par \uc2  He says buyers are not as interested in speculation as they are in arbitrage opportunities. \par \par \uc2  'The overnight (Hong Kong dollar) rate is between 5.0% and 5.5%, whereas in the U.S., it's about 6.0%. That means if you buy U.S. dollars and sell Hong Kong dollars, every day you'll gain,' the treasury chief says. 'People buying the 'TT' are not speculators. They're merely trying to earn interest on arbitrage.' \par \par \uc2  The treasurer says he thinks both short-term and forward speculators have been squeezed out of the market over the past month by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2 's aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and money markets. \par \par \uc2  After two weeks of heavy money market injections to make positions uncomfortable for position-sitting forward speculators, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  now appears to be encouraging slight movement in the Hong Kong dollar, the treasury chief says. \par \par \uc2  From the end of January, the territory's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  held to an extremely tight range between 7.7305 per U.S. dollar and 7.7315 per U.S. dollar while the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  attempted to halt speculation that drove the dollar to within HK$0.03 of its HK$7.8000 peg rate and steady the territory's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at stronger levels. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 01-03-95 \par \par \uc2  0415GMT \par \par \uc2  1053 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011025dr3201hvi\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
