\par\par \b \uc2  HK money rates close easier on HKMA injections. \b0\par\par\uc2 328 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Februar 1995\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1995 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  HONG KONG, Feb 16 (Reuter) - Interbank rates finished lower in response to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "There was two-way trading interest in the market, with the HKMA seen offering offering three-month cash earlier today," a Japanese bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  Overnight cash fell to 4-1/2 3/8 percent from 5-15/16 7/8 at Wednesday's close and three-month was at 6-15/16 13/16 percent against seven 6-15/16. \par \par \uc2  The authority injected a total of HK$2.26 billion into the banking system today. \par \par \uc2  The authority's moves were aimed to check the Hong Kong dollar's rise and to cool the overnight rate, which had been on the high side of the discount window offer rate of 6.25 percent, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The Hong Kong dollar rose to 7.7300 to the U.S. unit on an inflow of hot money for local stocks, \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders said. \par \par \uc2  Government paper was mixed with Exchange Fund bill yields down across the board but longer-dated notes yielding higher than their Wednesday closes, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  One-week and three-month bill yields fell in line with money rates, easing 10 and 5 points to 5.80 and 6.43 percent. \par \par \uc2  Yields of five year notes rose five basis points to 8.42 percent and three year gained one to 7.92 percent. \par \par \uc2  "Investors remain cautious amid the rising interest rate trend and they are looking at U.S. economic data and resposnes of the U.S. Treasuries for directions," a dealer said. \par \par \uc2  Bill yields should stay low on fears of HKMA \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  but notes might continue to fall on expected selling pressure. \par \par \uc2  The closing balance of the Exchange Fund was HK$2.15 billion after the HKMA injection and after banks deposited HK$1.38 billion at the discount window. - HK NEWSROOM (852) 843 6369 \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1995 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011104dr2g00z54\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
