\par\par \b \uc2  Bankers say HK's Bank of China mulling CD issue. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Alison Leung \par \uc2 586 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Februar 1995\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1995 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  HONG KONG, Feb 8 (Reuter) - Banking sources said the Bank of China (BoC) in Hong Kong is considering a certificate of deposit (CD) issue but is concerned about the immaturity and general slack condition of the market here. \par \par \uc2  "The local CD market is not perfect and there are still a lot of defects," a banker close to the BoC, China's state-owned foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bank, said. \par \par \uc2  "The BoC cannot issue CDs just because it want to have such an issue in the market," he added. \par \par \uc2  Banking sources said the BoC would make a decision next week on whether to issue the CDs. \par \par \uc2  They said market rumours suggested the bank was looking at an issue of up to HK$3.0 billion. \par \par \uc2  "There are internal discussions about the possibility of a CD issue but no decision has yet been made," the banker said. \par \par \uc2  He said there was no urgent need for the BoC to make a CD issue because its deposits exceeded loans. It would therefore have to decide on how to use any funds raised from such issues. \par \par \uc2  The BoC has not issued any CDs since November 1986 when its Macau branch launched a HK$100 million 5-year fixed rate CD. \par \par \uc2  In comparison with products such as equities, the secondary market for CDs is inactive. Only a limited number of banks participate in the CD market. \par \par \uc2  "If there was a better market, the BoC would have issued CDs last year," the banker said. \par \par \uc2  The response to ABN Amro Bank's HK$1.0 billion 5-year floating rate CD issue last month was thin, bankers said. \par \par \uc2  Only five banks participated, taking 39 percent of the issue leaving the four arrangers with HK$152.5 million each, they said. \par \par \uc2  Some bankers attributed the tepid response to pricing, which was set before the recent squeeze in the interbank market when the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong Monetary Authority intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  from January 12 to protect the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar and pushed short-term interbank rates to over 10 percent. \par \par \uc2  Rates moved down to around six percent in late January. \par \par \uc2  An arranger for a Citibank Floating Rate CD (FRCD) issue said the syndication of its HK$1.0 billion three- and five-year Floating Rate CDs had been extended for three more working days to February 15. \par \par \uc2  "It is normal that banks would take a more conservative approach after making substantial losses during the squeeze," a local banker said. \par \par \uc2  "They are still licking their wounds," he said. \par \par \uc2  An arranger for Generale Belgian Bank's current HK$1.0 billion issue said response to the issue was relatively slow. Allocation is expected on Monday. He attributed the slow response to a lack of appetite after several recent issues. \par \par \uc2  Hong Kong dollar-denominated capital market issues, mostly FRCDs, flooded the market last year, amounting to HK$55 billion, a 285 percent increase on the year. \par \par \uc2  Among them were the Hongkong and Shanghai Bank's three-year FRCD which raised HK$3.2 billion last September, followed by Hang Seng Bank's three-year HK$2.1 billion issue and Standard Chartered Bank's five-year HK$2.5 billion issue. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of East Asia also issued a HK$1.0 billion step-up bond arranged by Merrill Lynch. \par \par \uc2  "People will now think twice about whether it is sensible to acquire that many long-term facilities," the local banker said. \par \par \uc2  -- Hong Kong newsroom (852) 2843 6369 \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1995 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011103dr2800j5a\par }\page {\par\fs20{