\par\par \b \uc2  HK long-term interbank rates close higher. \b0\par\par\uc2 252 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Januar 1995\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1995 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  HONG KONG, Jan 18 (Reuter) - Interbank rates closed mixed after demand for swaps pushed long-term rates up, but the overnight rate eased slightly in late trade, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Overnight eased to 5-3/4 1/4 percent from 6-1/2 six, and three-month rose to 7-5/8 1/8 from 7-3/8 6-7/8. \par \par \uc2  "Some orders from Tokyo bought three- and six-month Hong Kong dollar futures in the swap market, which triggered some follow-through borrowings," a dealer at an American bank said. \par \par \uc2  The firmer term rates helped lift the local dollar to close at HK$7.7383/93 to the U.S. dollar vs HK$7.7445/55 at the open. A dealer also noted some corporate demand for local dollars. \par \par \uc2  Dealers hoped the market would return to more normal spreads, rates and volumes next week after the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2 's recent spate of \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in support of the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The authority took a total of HK$1.89 billion out of the market and the balance of the Exchange Fund was HK$1.12 billion after banks borrowed HK$887 million at the discount window. \par \par \uc2  Note trading was volatile, influenced by higher term rates. Five-year notes yielded as high as nine percent before retreating to 8.88 at the close against Tuesday's 8.81. Bill yields rose in respond to the cash rates but trading was quiet. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1995 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011103dr1i06zq7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
