\par\par \b \uc2  HK banks see interbank rates lower next week. \b0\par\par\uc2 209 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Januar 1995\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1995 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  HONG KONG, Jan 13 (Reuter) - Hong Kong interbank rates are expected to cool off next week after the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2 's (HKMA) \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  caused chaos in the credit markets, said Ian Wilson, chairman of the Hong Kong Association of Banks (HKAB) said. \par \par \uc2  "I think you'll see next week that we're back to normal rates," Ian Wilson told reporters after the HKAB's weekly meeting. \par \par \uc2  The overnight rate rose to almost 13 percent during the day after the HKMA withdrew funds from the banking system to prop up the Hong Kong dollar. \par \par \uc2  The overnight rate is normally below the discount window rate offered by the HKMA, which is currently 5.75 percent. Overnight cash finished the day at just over six percent. \par \par \uc2  When asked about the chaotic state of the interbank market, Wilson said: "I think it's the price you pay for a short term fix for something that was not a regular market feature." \par \par \uc2  "The best way to counter (speculation) is to be on your toes and to take action at that time when you see it happening," he said. \par \par \uc2  -- Hong Kong newsroom (852) 842 6441 \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1995 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011103dr1d0896h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
